<ui:UXML xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:ui="UnityEngine.UIElements" xmlns:uie="UnityEditor.UIElements" noNamespaceSchemaLocation="../../../UIElementsSchema/UIElements.xsd" editor-extension-mode="False">
    <ui:Template name="PortraitChip" src="project://database/Assets/UI/Components/PortraitChip.uxml?fileID=9197481963319205126&amp;guid=4bf9bc0d99c19974ba679757720cb1c3&amp;type=3#PortraitChip"/>
    <ui:Template name="ResourceBar" src="project://database/Assets/UI/Components/ResourceBar.uxml?fileID=9197481963319205126&amp;guid=44cca0b1ee5faf24ca8b7998fda1a8d8&amp;type=3#ResourceBar"/>
    <Style src="project://database/Assets/UI/Components/HudComponents.uss?fileID=7433441132597879392&amp;guid=9a6af0fb41c3869408a05ccb9e1313d1&amp;type=3#HudComponents"/>
    <!-- Shared unit frame (design 6.6 §3): portrait beside a stack of resource bars, composed from
         the same PortraitChip and ResourceBar every other surface uses.

         Salvaged from the retired Assets/UI/BattleUI.uxml — PlayerArea (lines 7-17) and
         EnemyInfoArea (lines 30-42) were the only enemy/ally frames ever drawn. They differ in
         exactly one way (the enemy mirrors: row-reverse, bars spread instead of centred), so the
         guardian/enemy variant is the enemy modifier class on the instance (see HudComponents.uss)
         rather than a second template. BattleUI.uxml's enemy telegraph block is NOT folded in: it is
         guardian-specific content, not part of a unit frame, and design 6.6 Q6 has not settled
         its shape yet.

         Per-instance configuration: portrait art via a portrait modifier class on the Portrait
         instance; bar identity via a .hpBar/.manaBar class on each ResourceBar instance. Bars are
         found from C# as Q<VisualElement>("HealthBar").Q<ProgressBar>("Bar").

         THE UNIT'S NAME LIVES IN THE STATS COLUMN, ABOVE THE BARS (Wrysk, 2026-07-29), not under
         the portrait. The portrait chip's own floating label is therefore hidden for every unit
         frame (see the unitFrame rule for it in HudComponents.uss) and the name is the UnitName
         Label below, which is what BattleUnitFrameBinder writes. Two reasons it had to be a
         template change rather than a stylesheet override: UXML has no slots, so a battle-side
         sheet cannot move the chip's label into a sibling column, and the chip's label is
         absolutely positioned over the button's corner, which is unreadable once a portrait
         stretches the whole height of the frame.

         NO CONSUMER YET — the world HUD has no unit frame (design 6.3 §4 moved HP off the HUD onto
         battle-arena units). Built now because 6.6 §5 work package 3 composes it and because this
         is where the BattleUI salvage has to land before that file is cleaned up. -->
    <ui:Instance template="PortraitChip" name="Portrait" class="portraitChip unitFrame__portrait"/>
    <ui:VisualElement name="Stats" class="unitFrame__stats">
        <ui:Label text="Unit" name="UnitName" picking-mode="Ignore" class="unitFrame__name"/>
        <ui:Instance template="ResourceBar" name="HealthBar" class="hpBar">
            <AttributeOverrides element-name="Bar" title="Health"/>
        </ui:Instance>
        <ui:Instance template="ResourceBar" name="ManaBar" class="manaBar">
            <AttributeOverrides element-name="Bar" title="Mana"/>
        </ui:Instance>
    </ui:VisualElement>
</ui:UXML>
